diagnosis_text:
A thorough four-phase sweep of the live Kath4 network found no fault of any kind. The user's report — that all Campus 2 machines (PC5–PC14, 172.16.x.x) have lost reachability to Campus 1 (10.5.x/10.6.x) due to router16's OSPF failing — is not supported by current evidence.

**OSPF control plane (Phase B):** All nine FRR routers are alive and healthy. Router16 in particular shows vtysh OK, both control sockets present, systemd active, and two Full OSPF adjacencies — one to mswitch0 (bond0, 10.2.2.x) and one to router7 (eth2, 172.30.1.x). Every router in the fabric, including mswitch0 and sshswitch, carries exactly 17 OSPF routes, indicating the full topology including 10.5.0.0/24 and 10.6.0.0/24 (advertised by sshswitch) is propagated correctly to all Campus 2 gateways.

**Data plane (Phase 1):** The topology-wide reachability sweep confirmed 0% loss on all tested cross-campus pairs, including Campus 2 sources pinging Campus 1 targets (10.6.0.x).

**Infrastructure (Phase A):** No ACL rules, no ARP poisoning, no addressing anomalies, no duplicate MACs; all 15 end-hosts hold correct IPs and default routes.

**Process layer (Phase D):** No flap scripts, stress tools, or resource pressure on any of the 28 devices — ruling out an intermittent fault with an active mechanism.

**Root cause:** No fault is present. The user's complaint almost certainly describes a past incident (router16 OSPF outage) that has since self-recovered, or was never active during this observation window. No corrective action is needed; the inter-campus routing is fully operational.
